WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
Thanks for tuning in to First Principles Daily, Episode 53.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.220
Let's break something down to its raw materials

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.960
and build the reasoning back up.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.180
For a century, every photograph

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.500
carried a real cost in silver, chemicals, and paper.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.700
Silicon sensors collapsed the marginal cost

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.400
of an image to nearly zero by turning photons into charge.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.520
Before digital sensors, taking a photograph meant consuming

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.380
a physical role of film whose emulsion contained

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.480
grains of silver halide that reacted to light.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.720
Each frame therefore carried a direct materials

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.360
and processing expense that photographers weighed

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.780
before pressing the shutter.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.460
Engineers who started instead from the physics of light

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.480
itself asked what was strictly required to record

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.440
a photon event.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.080
They replaced the consumable chemistry

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.260
with a reusable silicon array that converts photons

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.260
into electrons.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.340
That single change removed the per image cost entirely.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.240
The result was not merely cheaper pictures,

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.560
but a change in how often people chose

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.240
to record the world around them.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.600
For more than 100 years, the photographic industry

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.260
treated the capture of an image as inseparable

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.940
from the consumption of silver halide film,

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.300
liquid developers, fixers, and printing paper.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.300
Manufacturers and processors optimized

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.160
the existing chemical chain rather than questioning

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.540
whether chemistry was necessary at all.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.960
A typical 35 millimeter role held 36 exposures.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.740
After exposure, the film had to be developed in a dark room

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.280
or sent to a lab, then printed on light-sensitive paper

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.480
that itself contained silver compounds.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.400
The finished cost per usable image

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.800
therefore included the price of the film stock,

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.400
the chemicals, the paper, and the labor of processing

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.060
and printing.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.060
Photographers rationed shots because each additional frame

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.640
added a measurable increment to their expense.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.660
Professional studios and newspapers maintained dark rooms

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.740
and chemical inventories precisely

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.800
because the workflow had become the accepted infrastructure.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.740
Consumers accepted the same model

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.900
when they dropped off film at drug stores

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.040
and waited for prints.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.280
The convention persisted because every incremental improvement,

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.800
faster films, better papers, one hour processing,

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.240
still operated inside the consumable paradigm.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.240
No one inside the dominant supply chain

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.660
asked what the raw physical requirement of recording light

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.360
actually was once the constraint of chemistry was removed.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.820
The result was a stable industry

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.800
whose pricing reflected ongoing material throughput

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.340
rather than the underlying task of storing a pattern of photons.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.480
Even when color negative films replaced black and white stocks

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.880
or when instant cameras shortened the weight

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.500
between exposure and print, the core model stayed fixed.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.340
Each new image still required fresh silver halide crystals

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.680
suspended in gelatin, fresh baths of developer and fixer

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.700
and fresh sheets of enlarging paper.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.100
Studios tracked silver prices

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.640
because the metal formed the light sensitive heart

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.260
of every emulsion layer.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.960
A rise in commodity silver translated directly into higher film

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.360
and paper costs passed along to customers.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.740
The entire ecosystem of camera clubs, film magazines,

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.960
and retail photo counters reinforced the assumption

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.240
that photography was a craft whose economics were governed

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.640
by consumables rather than by the physics

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.300
of photon detection alone.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:32.060
Engineers at laboratories, including bell labs

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.820
and later at companies such as Kodak and Sony

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.340
began with the question of what physics actually demands

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:39.580
to register light.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.320
A photon striking certain materials

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:43.800
can liberate an electron.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.500
Silicon, an abundant semiconductor,

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.660
can be fabricated into an array of photo sites

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.840
that collect and hold that charge.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.380
Once the charge pattern is read out and digitized

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.660
the information can be stored in non-volatile memory

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.220
whose cost per bit has fallen for decades.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.440
The magic wand estimate for the raw materials

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.600
needed to record one additional image

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.100
is therefore only the incremental energy

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.540
and the tiny fraction of a silicon die

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.960
plus a few bits of flash storage.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.500
That estimate sits on the order of fractions of a cent

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.580
once the sensor and memory exist.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.920
In contrast, the conventional film workflow

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.120
carried a materials cost that was a substantial fraction

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.940
of the finished price of each print.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.700
The idiot index of the old system was therefore high

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.600
because the finished delivered image cost many times

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.080
the value of the silver and paper it consumed.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.340
To see why, consider that the price paid for a developed print

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.420
bundled the silver halide grains,

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.060
the gelatin carrier, the paper base,

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.320
the developer and fixer baths,

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.800
and the processing labor into one total.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.000
The raw silver and paper inside that total

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.960
formed only a modest slice of the final bill,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.840
leaving the rest as the cost of the consumable chain itself.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.780
That ratio signaled that the design,

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.340
not the material, was the real constraint.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.920
The first decisive redesign step was the shift

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.200
from film to charge coupled devices,

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.380
which eliminated the need to advance a new strip

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:17.300
of emulsion for every exposure.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.520
A second move replaced chemical development

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.640
with on-chip charge transfer and analog to digital conversion,

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.680
removing the entire darkroom supply chain.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.480
A third step introduced the Bayer color filter array

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.400
over the silicon photo sites,

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.620
allowing a single sensor to capture red, green,

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.480
and blue information without multiple exposures

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:40.760
or separate film layers.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:44.420
A fourth development moved storage from chemical paper

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:46.140
to solid state memory cards,

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.380
whose cost per gigabyte declined

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.440
along the semiconductor learning curve.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:54.340
Each of these changes attacked a different consumable layer.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.920
The silver halide itself, the processing baths,

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.360
the printing paper, and the physical archive.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.460
The key engineering trade-off at every stage

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.300
was maintaining sufficient signal to noise ratio

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.920
and dynamic range while removing the one-time use materials.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.820
Once the sensor and memory were in place,

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.300
the marginal cost of an extra exposure

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.180
fell to the cost of the electricity

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:19.400
to read the array and write the file.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.580
That cost sits close to the physical floor

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.980
set by the raw materials and the physics of charge collection.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:28.500
One objection sometimes raised

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.940
is that early digital sensors suffered from lower resolution

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:35.240
and poorer color fidelity than fine-grained film.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.080
The response was iterative improvement

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.100
in photosite density and micro lens design

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.560
rather than reversion to silver chemistry.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:47.240
Another objection concerns the capital intensity

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:51.480
of semiconductor fabs, yet that capital is spent once

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:53.960
to produce millions of reusable sensors,

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.740
whereas silver halide production required

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:59.540
continuous replenishment of metal and chemicals

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:00.760
for every new role.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:04.020
A further practical move was the integration

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:07.900
of the sensor with CMOS readout circuitry on the same die.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:11.140
That step reduced the number of discrete chips,

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:13.920
lowered power draw, and removed separate analog

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.300
to digital converter boards that had added cost and mass

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:19.680
in the first generation of digital backs.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.320
Each consolidation reduced leak paths for charge,

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.260
shortened signal paths, and cut assembly steps

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:29.400
that had previously been accepted as necessary overhead

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.660
in the transition from film scanners to integrated cameras.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:36.620
The transition produced a collapse in the cost per image

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.240
that made photography and ambient activity

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.760
rather than a budgeted one,

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.360
where a family might once have exposed

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:45.320
a few dozen frames on a vacation,

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.820
the same family now captures thousands without pausing

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:50.020
to consider materials.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:53.180
The new idiot index sits far lower

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:55.100
because the dominant remaining costs

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.200
are the one-time sensor, lens, and processor

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.340
rather than per shot consumables.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:04.840
Limits remain, high-quality sensors still require

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:06.920
precise semiconductor fabrication

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:09.060
whose capital cost is substantial,

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:11.560
and the energy cost of data centers

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.220
that store and transmit the resulting images is not zero.

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:18.660
Battery life, heat dissipation, and the optics

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:20.600
that focus light onto the sensor

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:22.680
continue to set practical boundaries.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:26.000
Nevertheless, the removal of the consumable

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.180
has already altered behavior at population scale.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:33.200
From citizen journalism to continuous scientific monitoring,

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.820
the shift also exposed new bottlenecks downstream.

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:39.900
The cost of reliable auto-focus motors,

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:42.120
image stabilization actuators,

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.520
and high bandwidth data interfaces inside the camera body

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.000
now represent larger fractions of the total bill of materials

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.980
than the sensor itself in many consumer devices.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.860
Yet these are still one-time components

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.520
whose performance improves along learning curves

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.500
rather than recurring per exposure.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.640
When a process is built around a consumable

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.120
that the underlying physics does not require,

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:08.720
the largest savings come from eliminating that consumable

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:10.560
rather than optimizing its use.

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:14.620
The second principle is that once the per-use cost approaches

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.560
the floor set by reusable materials and energy,

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:19.980
the constraint on human action shifts

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.460
from economics to attention and storage.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:25.180
The same move that turned photography

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:28.120
from a rationed activity into an always on one

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:31.720
now appears in other domains where consumables still dominate.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.500
Consider what happens when the next sensor or actuator

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:37.740
sheds its own recurring material cost.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.920
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:43.960
The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.020
Send this to a fellow builder.

